   "location": "reviews/Num/decision_relations.jsonl#M8-Num-REL-002",
   "description": "REL-002 states the ch.26 census merges applied the ch.1/ch.7 merge criterion \"with per-entry parashah preserved as counterevidence throughout.\" Verified against the four actual ch.26 merges (M8-Num-176 Simeon+Gad, M8-Num-179 Issachar+Zebulun, M8-Num-181 Manasseh+Ephraim, M8-Num-183 Benjamin+Dan+Asher): only M8-Num-183's frozen boundary_rationale explicitly restates the internal Masoretic markers as disclosed, non-overturning counterevidence (matching M8-Num-003 and M8-Num-052's pattern). M8-Num-176 and M8-Num-179 do not restate the internal setumah in their frozen boundary_rationale text; M8-Num-179's own original_language_primary reviewer flags this explicitly in evidence_refs as \"oshb-parashah:Num.26.25 setumah (internal, unrecorded)\" without escalating it to a formal challenge. M8-Num-181 discloses the override reasoning (setumah beaten by a verified inclusio) but not the marker itself as a counterevidence line. The marker evidence is present in every case in the underlying primary-review evidence_refs (nothing is lost from the record), and none of this affects the correctness of the boundary calls themselves, which stand independently on the content-identity test verified in each packet. This is a precision issue in the summary relation note's \"throughout\" claim, not a defect in the decisions it summarizes.",
   "affected_decisions": [
    "M8-Num-176",
    "M8-Num-179",
    "M8-Num-181"
   ],
   "recommended_action": "Soften REL-002's throughout phrasing, or restate the internal markers explicitly in M8-Num-176 and M8-Num-179's boundary_rationale to match M8-Num-183's fuller treatment. No status change recommended for the affected decisions; boundary calls are independently sound."
  }
